{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F25: an undeclared operand is dropped silently on the ingesting path",
    "description": "An equation references a name declared NOWHERE in the document. `esm validate` refuses it with a structural error naming the equation. `esm test` refuses it too -- on the ordinary path. On the INGESTING path it evaluates instead, drops the operand, and reports a plausible number.\n\nTHE REPRO IS TWO-SIDED, and that is what makes it worth filing rather than a wish. The check already exists and already says the right thing; one evaluation path does not reach it. Measured at the pinned toolchain on the same equation, the same undeclared name, and the same assertion:\n\n  * with `data_sources` REMOVED -- ERROR, `Unknown variable 'undeclaredFloor' referenced in expression`. Loud, named, unmissable.\n  * exactly as it stands here, ingesting one column the equation never reads -- `actual=2`, a clean run and a plausible answer.\n\nSo it is not that the toolchain cannot tell. It is that `max(known, undeclaredFloor)` becomes `max(known)` on one route, and the operand's disappearance is the only evidence that anything happened. This is the ninth instance of the failure class README's \"A warning about zeros\" enumerates, and the second found on the ingesting path after F24 -- which is the argument for looking there first rather than a coincidence: that path re-resolves names against a map built for the pipeline, and a name that map does not hold has, twice now, become an absence rather than an error.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND. `fixtures/nr-logging-county.esm` gained agedist.f's fold, whose running-total recurrence applies NONROAD's MINGRWIND floor as `max(running_total, minimumGrowthPopulation)`. The declaration did not come across with the equations. The fixture then reported 120 of 120 assertions passing -- including twelve end-to-end emission rows agreeing with the reference snapshot to 4e-06 -- while `esm validate` rejected the same bytes. The floor cannot bind on this data (the smallest base population on any equipment point is 0.5, against a floor of 1e-4), so its loss changed no digit HERE and would change the answer for a state with almost no equipment. Agreeing by accident on the data in front of you is the thing this repository is built to refuse, and only `run-tests.sh` running `validate` BEFORE `test` caught it.\n\nTHE ASSERTION BELOW IS DELIBERATELY NOT 2. Pinning 2 would pin the buggy answer and go green, which is the mistake this repro exists not to make. It is 10, the answer a floor of 10 would give. So it fails today as `actual=2 expected=10`, and on the day the ingesting path reaches the check it fails as an ERROR naming `undeclaredFloor`. Red either way until fixed, and the message says which stage caught it. THIS TEST IS EXPECTED TO FAIL.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "finding",
      "repro"
    ]
  },
  "domain": {
    "independent_variable": "t"
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "row": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 1
    },
    "step_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 4
    },
    "month_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "n_f24_runspecmonth"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "Undeclared": {
      "variables": {
        "known": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 2.0,
          "description": "The one operand that IS declared."
        },
        "clamped": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "row"
          ],
          "description": "max(known, undeclaredFloor). With the floor present and equal to 10, this is 10; with the floor silently absent it is `known`, which is 2. Both are plausible numbers and neither is flagged."
        },
        "m_monthID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "month_rows"
          ],
          "description": "runspecmonth.monthID. Read only so that the document INGESTS, and asserted only so that a failure cannot be blamed on the ingest not having happened.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "f24_runspecmonth",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "monthID"
            }
          }
        },
        "ingestedMonth": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "row"
          ],
          "description": "runspecmonth.monthID, summed over its one row, so the ingest can be asserted. Its only job is to prove this document really is on the ingesting path when the assertion above fails."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "_comment": "`undeclaredFloor` appears in no declaration block anywhere in this document. That is the whole repro.",
          "lhs": "clamped",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "i"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "i": {
                "from": "row"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "max",
              "args": [
                "known",
                "undeclaredFloor"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "ingestedMonth",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "i"
            ],
            "reduce": "+",
            "ranges": {
              "i": {
                "from": "row"
              },
              "k": {
                "from": "month_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "index",
              "args": [
                "m_monthID",
                "k"
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "an_undeclared_operand_is_an_error_not_an_absent_argument",
          "description": "The INTENDED behaviour. `esm test` should refuse this document exactly as `esm validate` does and as `esm test` itself does without the `data_sources` block, naming `undeclaredFloor`. It instead evaluates `max(known, undeclaredFloor)` as `known`. The ingested column `m_monthID` is asserted alongside so that a failure here can never be read as the ingest having gone wrong: the read succeeds, the document is genuinely on the ingesting path, and the recurrence-free equation next to it still loses an operand.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "clamped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 10.0,
              "coords": {
                "row": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "ingestedMonth",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 8.0,
              "coords": {
                "row": 1
              }
            }
          ],
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-06
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "data_sources": {
    "f24_runspecmonth": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "../../../moves.rs/characterization/snapshots/nr-logging-county/tables/db__movesexecution1ccc0232_campuscluster_illinois_edu__runspecmonth.parquet"
      },
      "extent": {
        "metaparameter": "n_f24_runspecmonth"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "tags": [
          "runspec"
        ],
        "esio_format": "parquet",
        "note": "One row, monthID 8. The smallest table in the snapshot, chosen because the point is that ANY ingest is enough -- the recurrence never reads it."
      }
    }
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "n_f24_runspecmonth": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in runspecmonth, discovered by the source's own extent. 1."
    }
  }
}